Trace:

  1. CurveConvex2Token._checkReentrancyContext() performs a regular external call
  (CurveConvexLib(payable(LP_LIB)).checkReentrancyContext()), not a delegatecall, so inside checkReentrancyContext the
  caller seen by the pool is the library instance itself. That is deliberate — the library's receive() comment
  explicitly says ETH arrives "to this contract ... during the checkReentrancyContext function."
  2. In the CurveInterface.V2 branch the library calls remove_liquidity(1, minAmounts, true, address(this)). A Curve v2
  crypto pool executes burnFrom(msg.sender, _amount) before computing amount = _amount - 1, so the call does require the
  library to hold ≥1 wei of the LP token. All LP-custody entrypoints (joinPoolAndStake, unstakeAndExitPool,
  transferYieldTokenToOwner) are delegatecalled into the vault (see _transferYieldTokenToOwner using _delegateCall), so
  at rest the library address holds zero LP. So far the mechanical revert claim is plausible.
  3. Where the claim breaks is its impact and its "permanence." The precondition the burn needs is nothing more than a 1
  wei balance of a freely transferable ERC20 at a known address. The Curve LP token has no transfer restrictions, is
  mintable by anyone via add_liquidity, and burnFrom on the Curve token performs no allowance check (only msg.sender ==
  minter, i.e. the pool). Therefore any liquidator — entirely unprivileged — can, in the same transaction, transfer 1
  wei of the LP token to LP_LIB and then invoke the liquidation; with the balance present, burnFrom(LP_LIB, 1) succeeds,
  amount = 1 - 1 = 0, every d_balance = balances[i] * 0 / total_supply = 0, so all min_amounts assertions (all zero)
  pass and the nonreentrant lock check completes normally. Nothing about the vault's state, the borrower's actions, or
  any privileged role can prevent that seeding.
  4. Consequently there is no state an unprivileged borrower can reach or maintain that makes the position "permanently
  unliquidatable." The borrower performs no action at all in the alleged exploit; the condition is a static property of
  a deployment-time interface selection, and it is clearable atomically, permissionlessly, at dust cost, by the very
  actor who wants to liquidate. At most this is a liquidator-ergonomics/dust-precondition issue, not a High-severity
  permanent liquidation brick.
  5. Under the tie-break, the claim's stated exploit path (permanent DoS of liquidation exploitable by an unprivileged
  borrower) does not hold as written.

  CONSTRAINT|class=C23|A denial-of-service claim must show that the blocking precondition cannot be restored by any
  permissionless, dust-cost action a third party can take atomically in the same transaction as the blocked call; if a
  freely transferable asset or public state can be seeded by the caller to satisfy the failing check, the claimed
  permanence and its severity collapse.
  VERDICT|REFUTED|src/single-sided-lp/CurveConvex2Token.sol:checkReentrancyContext:156|class=C23|The burn only needs a 1
  wei balance of an unrestricted, freely mintable/transferable LP token at the known library address, and the Curve
  token's burnFrom checks no allowance, so any liquidator can seed that dust in the same transaction (making amount = 1
  - 1 = 0, all payouts zero, all min-amount asserts pass) — the position is never permanently unliquidatable and no
  unprivileged borrower action creates or sustains the condition.
